amentals": {"margin": 2.02, "lean": -0.75, "environment": 4.35, "incumbency": 0.0, "money": -1.58}, "rating_margin": 0.0, "sigma_race": 5.0, "sigma_total": 5.44, "baseline_margin": -0.27, "analyst_adjustment": -0.23, "key_factors": ["Recent polls give Jackson a slim 1-2 point lead, while betting markets price Bottoms near 60%", "Jackson holds a massive fundraising and spending advantage, but Bottoms' late $23M surge and national Democratic money narrow it", "Open seat in an R+1 state with no incumbent on the ballot", "All eight expert ratings call the race a Tossup"], "rationale": "Every recent likely-voter poll shows Jackson narrowly ahead while prediction markets favor Bottoms, and eight raters unanimously call the race a Tossup; the forecast lands at near parity with Jackson a shade in front on the weight of the polling.", "watch": "Whether Bottoms' late fundraising surge closes the spending gap enough to move the polling trajectory, or turnout patterns in metro Atlanta and rural Georgia shift, could tip a margin this thin either way.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Brian Kemp (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Georgia's governor race is a genuine tossup to succeed term-limited Republican Brian Kemp. Polls through late October show Rick Jackson holding a narrow edge of roughly one to two points, with every recent survey of likely voters putting the race within the margin of error. Betting markets read the same contest differently, pricing Keisha Lance Bottoms near 60 percent on Democratic enthusiasm and her late fundraising surge.\n\nMoney is the starkest asymmetry: Jackson has raised and spent vastly more, while Bottoms has relied on a late donation surge and national Democratic support to stay competitive. With the state's partisan lean near even and analysts uniformly rating the contest a tossup, the forecast lands essentially at parity, with Jackson a shade ahead on current polling. Turnout in metro Atlanta and rural Georgia will likel